> Anyway these practical difficulties in playing made me thought of an
> alternative version of Nethack that would be even less graphical and more
> accessible. That is howabout turning it into an interactive fiction game?

Nethack already has some support for visually-impared players and
screen readers, though I don't know how well they work.

The game isn't really OOPy, I'm afraid, it's written entirely in C
with heavy use of global variables for referring to the player and map
data.  But there are some interesting code separations, such as game
logic from display code, that may yet help such a port.

I think something like this may almost be possible.  The most
difficult part would be translating the rich 2D environment
surrounding the player (you can be surrounded by monsters, but only
attack the eight right by you, then there's lining them up for wands,
maneuvering so that you get weak monsters in front of strong ones and
combat tactics, and probably a host of other considerations, etc.

I myself don't see how Nethack is even useably playable with a screen
reader, but apparently it is.  Does anyone here have information on
what this is like?  I suppose that could be useful in devising an
"interactive fiction" port.

